  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Thursday)\nIssued at 130 PM MDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\n19z surface analysis had remnants of warm front from the eastern \nDakotas into eastern CO, which has left the CWA dry as the focus \nfor precipitation has shifted well southeast of the CWA. Plenty of\nsunshine over the CWA as shortwave ridging pokes into the \nnorthern Plains per water vapour loop.\n\nTonight/Friday, shortwave over the southwest CONUS will race \nnortheast and gently brush the southeastern reaches of the CWA \nwith low PoPs. Southeasterly boundary layer flow later \ntonight/early Friday will support fog/low clouds east of the Black\nHills. Latest ensemble guidance continues the trend of yesterday \nwith further east movement for this wave, so confidence high most \nof the CWA will be dry Friday. Fly in ointment may be over the far\nsouthwest where a sliver of weak buoyancy suggests unmentionable \nPoPs at this time with CAM support minimal. Temperatures will be \nnear guidance.\n\nFor the weekend, southwest flow aloft and thermal ridge will \nsupport high temperatures 5-10F above normal along with dry \nweather given little moisture availability. An upper trough will \napproach the northern Plains Sunday night through Tuesday with \nmodest moisture return and sufficient forcing for good chances of \nshra/TS. Machine learning guidance suggests potential for some \nactive/strong storms Sunday night/Monday. Temperatures will become\nseasonal as associated cold front passes through. Long range \nguidance suggests zonal, mostly dry flow with slightly above \nnormal temperatures.",
